🔹 What is Concurrency in AWS Lambda?
Concurrency means how many Lambda function instances are running at the same time.

Each time your Lambda is triggered, AWS creates a separate execution environment (like a small container) to run it.

If multiple requests come at the same time, Lambda runs multiple copies (instances) of your function in parallel.

👉 So concurrency = number of requests being served at the same time.

🔹 Example
Imagine you have a Lambda function that resizes an image:

🧑 User 1 uploads a photo → Lambda instance #1 runs

🧑 User 2 uploads a photo at the same time → Lambda instance #2 runs

🧑 User 3 uploads another photo → Lambda instance #3 runs

If 10 users upload images at the same time, then 10 Lambdas run in parallel.
👉 That means concurrency = 10.

🔹 Key Points
Default concurrency limit (per region) = 1,000 concurrent executions.

This means at most 1,000 instances of your Lambda can run at the same time.

If more requests come than the concurrency limit → extra requests are throttled (delayed or rejected).

Concurrency is dynamic:

If you get 1 request → only 1 instance runs.

If you get 500 requests at the same second → Lambda automatically scales up to 500 instances.

🔹 Types of Concurrency Controls
Unreserved concurrency → By default, all your functions share the account limit (like 1,000).

Reserved concurrency → You can reserve concurrency for a function (e.g., 100). That function will always have capacity, but it cannot exceed 100.

Provisioned concurrency → Keeps some Lambda instances warm and ready all the time to reduce cold start delay.

🔹 Real-Life Example
Think of Lambda like a restaurant kitchen 🍽️:

Each cook = one Lambda instance

Each customer order = one request (event trigger)

If 5 orders come in → 5 cooks start working (concurrency = 5).

If 1,000 orders come in → up to 1,000 cooks work in parallel.

If more than 1,000 orders come → extra customers wait (throttling).

✅ In short:
AWS Lambda concurrency = the number of function instances running at the same time.
It helps Lambda scale automatically with demand, but you need to manage limits to avoid throttling.

