Throttling (synchronous):

5 counters available, but 10 customers come at once.

5 are served, the other 5 are told “❌ come back later” (rejected).

Throttling (asynchronous):

5 counters available, 10 customers come.

5 are served, the other 5 are told “⌛ wait in retry line, we’ll call you again” (retried later).

Queuing (SQS/SNS):

Customers put their forms in a drop box (queue).

Bank clerks (Lambda) pick them up one by one whenever free.

Nobody gets rejected — forms just wait in the box.

✅ In short:

Throttling = requests exceed concurrency → rejected or retried (depending on sync/async).

Queuing = requests are stored in a queue (SQS/SNS/EventBridge) until Lambda has capacity to process them.

